readings.c1s3:
{{smaller|forked tongue of a snake has been invested by popular imagination with a stinging and poisonous action; but it is quite harmless, and serves chiefly as a feeler. (See also cut under ''snake.'')}}

{{smaller block|Every one that lappeth of the water with his ''tongue,'' as a dog lappeth, him shalt thou set by himself. {{right|Judges vii. 5.}}}}

'''2.''' Specifically, in ''cookery,'' a beef's tongue prepared for the table: as, smoked ''tongue.''—'''3.''' In ''conch.,'' the lingual ribbon, or odontophore, bearing the radula, or rasping surface, a structure highly characteristic of those mollusks which have heads, as gastropods. See the technical names (with cuts under ''radula'' and ''ribbon'').—'''4.''' In ''entom.,'' some mouth-part or conformation of mouth-parts serving as a tongue or suggesting one; a proboscis; a haustellum; an antlia: as, the long spirally rolled ''tongue'' of a butterfly or moth; specifically, the central lobe of the ligula of a mandibulate insect. See the technical words, and cut under ''haustellum.''—'''5.''' In various figurative uses, the faculty or mode of speech; speech. {{smaller|(''a'') The faculty or power of speech; capacity of expression.}}

{{smaller block|The better ''tonge'' she hadde, ffor she was of all the worlde the feirest speker and the beste. {{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), ii. 322.}}
O, helpe thou my weake wit, and sharpen my dull ''tong!'' {{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., I., Prol., st. 2.}}
But the ''tongue'' can no man tame; it is an unruly evil, full of deadly poison. {{right|Jas. iii. 8.}}
This our life exempt from public haunt
Finds ''tongues'' in trees, books in the running brooks. {{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, ii. 1. 16.}}}}

{{smaller|(''b'') The act or habit of speaking; utterance; discourse; sometimes, fluency of speech; talk.}}

readings.c1s4:
{{smaller block|Use more respect, and, woman, 'twill become you;
At least, less ''tongue.'' {{right|''Fletcher'', Double Marriage, iv. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|Don't be sparing of your Speech with one that is full of ''Tongue.'' {{right|''N. Bailey'', tr. of Colloquies of Erasmus, I. 116.}}}}

(''c'') The manner of speaking as regards sound; voice; tone; specifically, in sporting language, the voice of a hound or other dog: as, to give ''tongue.''

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|With soft low ''tongue'' and lowly courtesy.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., Ind., i. 114.}}}}

{{smaller block|Every muse shall join her tuneful ''tongue.''
{{right|''Burns'', Death of Sir J. H. Blair.}}}}

{{smaller block|The ''tongue'' [of the bloodhound should be] loud, long, deep, and melodious.
{{right|''Dogs of Great Britain and America'', p. 56.}}}}

{{smaller|(''d'') The character of speech with regard to meaning or intention.}}

{{smaller block|Be of fair beerynge & of good ''tunge.''
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 37.}}}}

{{smaller block|Speak to me home, mince not the general ''tongue'':<br>Name Cleopatra as she is call'd in Rome.
{{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., i. 2. 109.}}}}

{{smaller|(''e'') The mode or form of expression; especially, the sum of the words used by a particular nation; a language.}}

{{smaller block|Reuertere is as myche to say<br>In englisch ''tunge'' as turne aȝen.
{{right|''Hymns to Virgin'', etc. (E. E. T. S.), p. 92.}}}}

{{smaller block|We must be free or die, who speak the ''tongue''<br>That Shakspeare spake.
{{right|''Wordsworth'', Poems on Independence and Liberty, xvi.}}}}

{{smaller|(''f'') Words or declarations only; mere speech or talk, as opposed to ''thoughts'' or ''actions.''}}

{{smaller block|Let us not love in word, neither in ''tongue'', but in deed and in truth. {{right|1 John iii. 18.}}}}

{{smaller|(''g'') A people or race, as distinguished by its language.}}

{{smaller block|I will gather all nations and ''tongues.'' {{right|Isa. lxvi. 18.}}}}

{{smaller|(''h''†) Mention; fame; eulogy.}}

{{smaller block|She was born noble; let that title find her a private grave, but neither ''tongue'' nor honour. {{right|''Beau. and Fl.''}}}}

{{smaller|(''i'') A vote; a voice. [Rare.]}}

{{smaller block|Of [on ?] him that did not ask, but mock, [do you] bestow<br>Your sued-for ''tongues''? {{right|''Shak.'', Cor., ii. 3. 216.}}}}

6. Anything considered to resemble an animal's tongue in shape, position, or function.

readings.c2s2:
¶
{{smaller block|This is known as the North Deposit, and is separated by a ''tongue'' of barren dolomite from another ore-bearing portion.
{{right|''Ure'', Dict., IV. 1004.}}}}

{{smaller block|Columns with richly carved capitals, and, like so many columns of all ages in this region, with ''tongues'' of foliage at their bases.
{{right|''E. A. Freeman'', Venice, p. 207.}}}}

Especially—(''a'') A long narrow strip of land running out into a sea or lake; also, a gulf or outstretched bay (Isa. xi. 15). (''b'') A tapering jet of flame. (''c'') The pin or tang of a buckle or brooch which pierces the strap, ribbon, or object to be fastened. (''d'') The short movable rail of a switch by which the wheels are directed to one or the other line of rails. (''e'') The pole of a carriage, car, or other vehicle, to which the horses are fastened. (''f'') A projecting strip worked on the edge of a board, used to form a joint by fitting into a corresponding groove in another board. (''g'') The pointer or pin of a balance. See cut under ''balance.'' (''h''†) ''Naut.'', a short piece of rope spliced into the upper part of standing backstays to form an eye; also, the upper piece of a built mast. (''i'') The vibratile reed of a musical instrument of the reed group, particularly if made of metal, as in the harmonium, the concertina, etc. Compare cuts under ''reed.'' (''j'') The clapper of a bell. (''k'') That part of the blade of a sword on which the grip, shell, and pommel are fixed. (''l'') A narrow strip of leather or kid, over which the uppers or sides of a boot or shoe are laced together. (''m'') A young or small sole. Compare ''tongue-fish. Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]

{{smaller block|The average weight of the fish has diminished. Young specimens form the majority of the soles in the market, and are sold under the names of "slips" or "''tongues.''"
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XXII. 249.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
(''n'') The sting of a bee. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.] (''o'') The movable arm of a bevel, the principal member being the stock, which forms the case when the instrument is closed. ''E. H. Knight.'' See cut under ''bevel.'' (''p'') A current of water, narrow, deep, and smooth, running rapidly between rocks without breaking or twisting; a sled-run. A tongue is well-known to anglers as a favorite resting-place of salmon in their laborious ascent of rapid streams.

7. One of the seven (later eight) divisions or “nations” composing the order of the Hospitalers; also, a meeting of a division.—'''A long tongue.''' See ''long''<sup>1</sup>.—'''A tongue too long for one's teeth''', an overready or indiscreet tongue. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|Hum! Eve, wasn't your ''tongue'' a little ''too long for your teeth'' just now? {{right|''C. Reade'', Love me Little, x.}}}}

'''Auld wives' tongues.''' See ''auld.''—'''Black tongue.''' (''a'') An affection characterized by a discoloration, at first black, fading later into brown, of the filiform papillæ of the tongue. Also called ''nigritis linguæ.'' (''b'') A fever which prevailed in the western United States in the winter of 1842–3. ''Dunglison.'' (''c'') An inflammation of the tongue occurring in some forms of epidemic erysipelas.—'''Confusion of tongues''', according to the account in Gen. xi., a confusion of speech inflicted on the builders of the tower of Babel, resulting in their dispersion: generally regarded as the first occasion of a difference of languages.—'''Double-tongue.''' See ''Ruscus.''—'''Egg and tongue.''' See ''egg''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Excision of the tongue.''' See ''Chassaignac's'', ''Jacque's'', ''Nunneley's'', ''Regnoli's'', ''Roux's'', and ''Whitehead's operations for excision of the tongue'', under ''operation.''—'''Gift of tongues.''' See ''gift.''—'''Liguliform tongue.''' See ''liguliform.''—'''Mother tongue.''' See ''mother-tongue.''—'''On''' (or '''at''') '''the tip''' (or '''end''') '''of one's tongue''', on the point or verge of utterance.

{{smaller block|God forgive me, but I had a sad lie ''at my tongue's end.'' {{right|''Richardson'', Pamela, I. 169.}}}}

{{smaller block|It was ''on the tip of'' the boy's ''tongue'' to relate what had followed; but . . . he checked himself.}}

readings.c2s4:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Dickens'', Martin Chuzzlewit, xxix.}}}}

'''Raphe of the tongue.''' See ''raphe.''—'''Strawberry tongue.''' See ''strawberry.''—'''The tongue of the trump''', the tongue of a jews'-harp; hence, the most important person or thing. [Scotch.]

{{smaller block|An' there will be black-lippit Johnnie,
''The tongue o' the trump'' to them a'.
{{right|''Burns'', Election Ballads, ii.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
¶

'''The tongues,''' foreign languages.

{{smaller block|In turning over those same leaves apace,<br>To shew his skill i' ''th' tongues,'' hee'l nod his head.
{{right|''Times' Whistle'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 29.}}
What is "pourquoi"? do or not do? I would I had bestowed that time in ''the tongues'' that I have in fencing, dancing, and bear-baiting.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., i. 3. 97.}}}}

'''To bite the tongue.''' See ''bite.''—'''To find one's tongue,''' to be able to speak; recover the power of speech.

{{smaller block|But Priam ''found'' the fire ere he ''his tongue.''
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., i. 1. 74.}}}}

'''To give tongue.''' See ''give''<sup>1</sup>.—'''To hold one's tongue.''' See ''hold''<sup>1</sup>.—'''To keep one's tongue†,''' to be silent.

{{smaller block|When Biondello comes, he waits on thee;<br>But I will charm him first to ''keep his tongue.''
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., i. 1. 214.}}}}

'''Tongue-and-groove joint.''' See cut under ''joint,'' 1 (''e'').—'''Tongue-scapular.''' See ''scapular.''—'''To throw tongue,''' to give tongue, as dogs.—'''To wag one's (the) tongue,''' to speak or talk: used in contempt.

{{smaller block|What have I done, that thou darest ''wag thy tongue''<br>In noise so rude against me? ''Shak.'', Hamlet, iii. 4. 39.}}

'''Wooden tongue.''' See the quotation.

{{smaller block|In cattle the disease [actinomycosis] manifests itself by firm tumours in the jaw, in the alveoli of the teeth, and particularly by a great enlargement and induration of the tongue—''wooden tongue.''
{{right|''E. Klein'', Micro-Organisms and Disease, p. 148.}}}}

='''Syn.''' 5 (''e''). ''Tongue'' is the Anglo-Saxon equivalent for ''language.'' See ''language.''

'''tongue''' (tung), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''tongued'', ppr. ''tonguing.'' [< ''tongue'', ''n.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' 1. To chide; scold; reproach.

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|I'll listen to the common censure now,
How the world ''tongues'' me when my ear lies low.
{{right|''Middleton'', Michaelmas Term, iv. 4.}}}}
2. To speak; utter.
{{smaller block|'Tis still a dream, or else such stuff as madmen
''Tongue'' and brain not.
{{right|''Shak.'', Cymbeline, v. 4. 147.}}}}
{{smaller block|No stone is fitted in yon marble girth
Whose echo shall not ''tongue'' thy glorious doom.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Tiresias.}}}}
3. In playing on musical wind-instruments, to modify or interrupt the tone of by means of a stroke of the tongue, so as to produce a marcato or staccato effect, as in the flute, the cornet, etc. See ''tonguing''. Also ''tip''.—4. To join or fit together by means of a tongue and groove. See the phrase.—{{smaller|'''Tonguing and grooving''', a mode of joining boards by forming a groove or channel in one board, and a corresponding projection on the edge of the other, which is fitted into the first. Planes are used in pairs to form these grooves and projections respectively. Also called ''grooving and feathering'', ''plowing and tonguing''.}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' 1. To talk; prate: with indefinite ''it''.
{{smaller block|Let his clack be set a-going, and he shall ''tongue it'' as impetuously and as loudly as the arrantest hero of the play.
{{right|''Dryden'', Pref. to Troilus and Cressida.}}}}
{{smaller block|Our Captain dared the sachem to come out and fight him like a man, showing how base and woman-like he was in ''tonguing it'' as he did.
{{right|''Good News from New England'' (Appendix to New England's Memorial, p. 373).}}}}
2. In ''music'', to use the tongue for the purpose of modifying sounds in playing the flute and some other wind-instruments.—3. To run out; project: as, a point of land ''tongues'' out into the sea.

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|Old icebergs bulge and ''tongue'' out below, and are thus prevented from uniting.{{right|''Kane'', Sec. Grinn. Exp., I. 282.}}}}

'''tongue-bang''' (tung′bang), ''v. t.'' To scold heartily. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]

'''tongue-banger''' (tung′bang″ėr), ''n.'' A scold. [Prov. Eng.]

{{smaller block|That Sally she turn'd a ''tongue-banger'', an' räated ma.{{right|''Tennyson'', Northern Cobbler.}}}}

'''tongue-battery''' (tung′bat″ėr-i), ''n.'' Urgent and pressing talk; a flood of words. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|With blandish'd parlies, feminine assaults,<br>''Tongue-batteries'', she surceased not, day nor night,<br>To storm me.{{right|''Milton'', S. A., l. 404.}}}}

'''tongue-bird''' (tung′bėrd), ''n.'' The long-tongue or wryneck, ''Iynx torquilla'': so called from the long extensile tongue. See cut under ''wryneck''.

'''tongue-bit''' (tung′bit), ''n.'' A form of bit for a hard-mouthed horse, with a plate so fixed that the horse cannot get his tongue over the mouthpiece.

'''tongue-bone''' (tung′bōn), ''n.'' The hyoid bone, or os hyoides. See cuts under ''hyoid'' and ''skull''.

'''tongue-case''' (tung′kās), ''n.'' In ''entom.'', that part of the integument of a pupa which covers the tongue. {{smaller|It is seen in many chrysalids, and in the pupa of the sphinx-moth it forms a curved appendage like the handle of a pitcher.}}

'''tongue-chain''' (tung′chān), ''n.'' One of the chains which support the fore end of a wagon-tongue and connect it with the hames of the harness.

'''tongue-compressor''' (tung′kom-pres″or), ''n.'' A clamp for holding down the tongue during dental operations on the lower jaw.
